---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href, tagHref } from '../lib/notes';
import { SITE_FAMILY, SITE_TITLE, TAGS } from '../lib/site';

const others = SITE_FAMILY.filter((s) => s.title !== SITE_TITLE);
---

<Base title="About" description="Whose notebook this is, what its tags cover, and why it’s called the 3-way distiller." current="about">
  <article class="entry">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I’m Sungmin. This is my notebook, kept in public mostly so that I
        write things down properly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The notes fall under three tags: past, present and future.
      </p>
      <ul class="eras">
        {TAGS.map((t) => (
          <li>{t.era && <><span class="label">{t.era}</span> </>}<a href={tagHref(t.name)}>{t.name}</a>: {t.blurb}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        The name comes from the kind of distillation that turns up in all three: separating
        mixtures in a column, training a small model to imitate a large one, and distilling many
        noisy entangled pairs into a few good ones. All three are the same move. Take something
        complex and keep only what matters. That is also what I try to do in each entry here.
      </p>
    </div>

    {others.length > 0 && (
      <section class="about-block">
        <h2 class="label">Elsewhere</h2>
        <ul>
          {others.map((s) => (
            <li><a href={s.url}>{s.title}</a>: {s.blurb}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    )}

    <section class="about-block about-block-quiet">
      <h2 class="label">How to read this</h2>
      <p>
        Entries marked <span class="rough">rough</span> are unfinished thinking and
        may be wrong. Longer entries open with a short distillate, so you can stop there.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href={href()}>← index</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
